// Shared call_edge derivation rules.
//
// Include this file after declaring `.decl call_edge(caller: symbol, callee: symbol)`.
// Requires base.dl relations: remote_call, bif_call, local_call, function_def,
// closure_def. NOT instruction — see below.

// Remote call: caller function -> "mod:func/arity" string.
call_edge(caller_func, callee) :-
  remote_call(_, caller_func, mod, func, arity),
  callee = cat(mod, cat(":", cat(func, cat("/", to_string(arity))))).

// BIF call: caller function -> "mod:func/arity".
call_edge(caller_func, callee) :-
  bif_call(_, caller_func, mod, func, arity, _),
  callee = cat(mod, cat(":", cat(func, cat("/", to_string(arity))))).

// Local call: target is an MFA string matching a function_def's func field.
call_edge(caller_func, target) :-
  local_call(_, caller_func, target, _),
  function_def(target, _, _, _, _).

// Each of the three rules above used to recover its caller by joining
// `instruction(id, caller_func, _, _)`. That made `instruction` — 343k rows
// on a 531-module project, and rewritten whenever any function body changes,
// because an instruction ID is a raw per-function offset — an input to stage
// 0, and therefore to every analysis downstream of the call graph.
//
// The call relations now carry `caller` directly. Stage 0's inputs are the
// relations that describe what a function CALLS, so its input set is now as
// stable as its output, which is what this file's docstring always claimed
// for the output alone.

// Closure construction is treated as a static call edge so that call_reachable
// follows execution into closures passed to higher-order callees like Enum.map,
// :telemetry.span, Task.async, and Phoenix.PubSub callbacks. The Elixir compiler
// lifts closure bodies into top-level functions and references them via make_fun3,
// so they show up here as concrete function IDs.
call_edge(parent, child) :- closure_def(parent, child).
